import { EffectSequencingCopy } from "../model"

pub fn copy -> EffectSequencingCopy = EffectSequencingCopy {
  summary: "An ordinary LoopControl value allowing the next item to be processed.",
  taskTitle: "Move to the next item when this one is finished",
  purpose: "Use Continue to say that, after processing one item, traversal may proceed if more input remains. The example stores this value as keepGoing and contrasts it with the successful-stop decision, Break.",
  typescriptBefore: "The TypeScript example treats continue and break as string values. Passing a label named continue is different from executing a continue statement inside a loop. Here, the program only describes the values.",
  benefit: "Making the decision a callback's successful value lets the next item wait until this item's operation has finished. A callback can communicate that decision through its result.",
  setup: "Import std/effect as effects. effects.Continue is one of the two values of effects.LoopControl. The other value, effects.Break, describes a decision to finish the traversal successfully.",
  after: [
    "let keepGoing: effects.LoopControl = effects.Continue stores an ordinary value representing continuation. The annotation after : gives its type. stopHere has the same type but stores the decision to stop successfully.",
    "describe is an ordinary function using match to distinguish the value received as control. Continue selects the description permitting the next item, and Break selects the description of successful stopping. -> String says the function returns a string.",
    "effects.succeed stopHere demonstrates wrapping a plain value in an operation that succeeds with it. A continuing callback can use effects.succeed effects.Continue in the same way. Continue itself does not construct an operation.",
    "The do block in effect fn main performs output in order. _ <- println discards the completed output result. result <- effects.succeed stopHere receives its successful value, which is passed to describe before the final string is printed.",
    "This example only prints three descriptions. In an actual forEachUntil traversal, the callback operation must finish successfully with Continue before the next item is processed. An asynchronous callback is also awaited; items do not start concurrently.",
    "Continue does not retry the current item. If no input remains, traversal finishes successfully. forEachUntil's successful result is Unit, a completion-only value, not an array collecting Continue values or other per-item results."
  ],
  details: [
    "Merely creating Continue does not skip later statements. Do not treat it as an immediate jump like TypeScript's continue statement. Return the continuing operation as the callback result at the point where the next item should be permitted.",
    "Returning a bare value where the callback needs an operation is a mistake. Wrap it with effects.succeed effects.Continue. Conversely, when you only want to keep the value, use let as keepGoing does here.",
    "A previous Continue does not allow traversal to ignore a later failure. forEachUntil stops with failure if a callback fails, or stops successfully if it returns Break. After Break it does not pull another item."
  ]
}
